      "source": [
        "## Problem 1: Representing SHA-256 Data\n",
        "\n",
        "Explain and demonstrate suitable Python representations for:\n",
        "- 32-bit words;\n",
        "- sequences of 32-bit words;\n",
        "- input messages (`bytes`);\n",
        "- 512-bit message blocks;\n",
        "- the final 256-bit digest;\n",
        "- big-endian integers.\n",
        "\n",
        "
32-bit words: A word is a number that fits in 32 bits. In python, use a normal integer from 0 to 2*32 − 1. Use the mask 0xFFFFFFFF to cut off extra bits.
Lists of words: Use a Python list to hold many words. Each item is one word. It is easy to get or change any word.
Input message (bytes): The message is stored as Python bytes. This is a row of 8-bit values. SHA-256 reads the message one byte at a time.
512-bit blocks: SHA-256 splits the message into blocks of 512 bits. That is 64 bytes, or 16 words. Store one block as a list of 16 integers.
The final hash: The result is 256 bits. You can store it as 32 bytes or as a 64-character hex string. Each hex character is 4 bits.
Big-endian: This means the biggest byte goes first. In Python, use int.from_bytes(data, byteorder='big') to turn bytes into a number this way."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "
# Problem 1: Demonstrating 

# 1. Representing a 32bit word
word = 0x12345678
mask = 0xFFFFFFFF

word = word & mask

print("1. 32-bit word:")
print(hex(word))
print("Number of bits:", word.bit_length())


# 2. Representing a sequence of 32 bit words
words = [0x12345678, 0xABCDEF01, 0x000000FF]

print("\n2. Sequence of 32-bit words:")
print(words)


# 3. Representing an input message using bytes
message = b"Hello SHA-256"

print("\n3. Input message:")
print(message)
print("Message length:", len(message), "bytes")


# 4. Representing a 51 bit message block

block = [0] * 16

print("\n4. 512-bit message block:")
print(block)
print("Number of words:", len(block))
print("Total bits:", len(block) * 32)


# 5. Representing 256-bit digest
digest = bytes(32)
digest_hex = digest.hex()

print("\n5. Final 256-bit digest:")
print(digest)
print("Hexadecimal:", digest_hex)
print("Hexadecimal characters:", len(digest_hex))


# 6. Converting bytes into a big endian integer
data = bytes([0x12, 0x34, 0x56, 0x78])

big_endian_value = int.from_bytes(data, byteorder='big')

print("\n6. Big-endian integer:")
print(hex(big_endian_value))
"
